{% block content %}
<p>Before any LLM shows up, we need somewhere to put what it invents. The whole
world is a handful of Pydantic models:</p>

{{ snippet("world.py", "models") }}

<h2>Design choices worth noticing</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Open-ended dictionaries.</strong> <code>properties</code> on everything
      and <code>status</code> on the player are free-form <code>dict[str, str]</code>.
      The model can record <code>{"on_fire": "yes"}</code> or
      <code>{"form": "a sentient fog"}</code> without us ever predicting it. This is what
      makes "go crazy" possible with a tiny schema.</li>
  <li><strong>Location is a single field.</strong> A thing's <code>location</code> is the
      player (<code>"player"</code>), a place, a being, or another thing. Contents are
      <em>derived</em> by scanning, so there's no second list that can get out of sync.</li>
  <li><strong>Readable ids.</strong> The LLM has to refer to things by id, and
      <code>rusty-key</code> is much easier for it (and you) than a UUID:</li>
</ul>

{{ snippet("world.py", "unique_id") }}

<h2>Friendly aliases</h2>
<p>LLMs make small mistakes with ids. We accept <code>"here"</code> and
<code>"player"</code>, and when an id is wrong we raise an error that tells the model
<em>how to fix it</em>. You'll see why that matters on the tools page.</p>

{{ snippet("world.py", "resolve_location") }}

<h2>Search without embeddings</h2>
<p>The model must search before inventing (to avoid five different "old wizards").
A keyword score is plenty for a world of a few hundred entries:</p>

{{ snippet("world.py", "search") }}

<h2>Saving</h2>
<p>One JSON file per world. Pydantic does all the work:</p>
{{ snippet("world.py", "store") }}

<div class="box try">Play a few turns, then open <code>saves/&lt;world&gt;.json</code>.
Find the things the Dungeon Master invented.</div>

<div class="box exercise">Add a <code>weight</code> field to <code>Thing</code>? Don't!
Try asking the Dungeon Master to track weight via <code>properties</code> instead and
